\begin{multline}
    \bigl(s^{\tt int}, s^{\tt det}, d^{\tt int}, d^{\tt det}\bigr) \triangleq \\
    \bigl(s(\tau_{\tt int}),\, s(\tau_{\tt det}),\, d(\tau_{\tt int}),\, d(\tau_{\tt det})\bigr)
\end{multline}

We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\citep{qwen25-2024} (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The framework is applicable to any environment where paired episodes under two computation modes can be collected with binary success/failure indicators. We apply it unchanged to ALFWorld and MATH in \S\ref{sec:math-contrast}--\ref{sec:cross-env}.

\subsection{Episode-Level Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

We define a labeling function $L$ that maps a counterfactual pair to a routing preference:
\begin{multline}
    L\!\bigl(\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}, \tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}\bigr) \;\rightarrow \\
    \ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref},\; {\tt int\text{-}pref},\; {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}
\end{multline}

The labeling function is \emph{death-aware}: it prioritizes survival over score, then compares scores among same-status episodes (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}; Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}). ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline; label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_overview.pdf}
\caption{Counterfactual episode collection and analysis pipeline. Each task instance is run under both pure strategies independently, producing episode-level labels via Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we decompose the mean score gap between strategies. Let $\delta = \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt int}] - \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt det}]$ denote the death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ the mean score gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy dies. Partitioning episodes by death status, death-asymmetric episodes (where only one strategy dies) contribute a per-episode gap of $P + \bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$, where $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$ is the surviving strategy's mean score; when $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} \ll P$ (in our data, $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} = 0.87$, or $0.9\%$ of $P$), these episodes contribute ${\approx}\,\delta P$ in aggregate. The full gap thus decomposes as:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \underbrace{\delta \cdot P}_{\text{death contribution}} + \Delta_{\text{nd}}
\end{equation}
The approximation residual is $29.60 - 29.37 = 0.23$ ($0.8\%$ of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

We define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) as the fraction of the full score gap attributable to death avoidance:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}} \approx \frac{\delta P}{\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}}
\end{equation}%
\footnote{When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (deterministic strategy scores lower than internal on non-death episodes), DAF exceeds 1; death avoidance then overcompensates for a non-death disadvantage.}

Since DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$, it increases monotonically with $P$, a mathematical consequence rather than an empirical finding. We therefore report both DAF at the environment default ($P{=}100$) and the penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction as complementary metrics. In ScienceWorld, $P$ affects only post-hoc score calculation; the agent's policy (ReAct prompting) does not include $P$ in its observation, so death rates and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ are $P$-invariant by design (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}).

\paragraph{Statistical inference.}
We quantify uncertainty via episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$). Each resample draws $N = 245$ episodes with replacement, recomputes all metrics, and derives percentile-based 95\% CIs. We resample at the episode level (not the step level) because the episode is the unit of counterfactual comparison. Penalty sensitivity analysis across hypothetical $P$ values is in Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}.

We also measure whether death-avoidance signal is concentrated in a few task types via a Signal Concentration Index (SCI $\in [0, 1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}), reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\paragraph{Scope of episode-level analysis.}
At step level, 97.4\% of oracle comparisons yield ties (\S\ref{sec:collection}); of 2{,}040 training steps, only 0.3\% exhibit strict directional conflict with episode labels, with 60.3\% pre-event neutral (neither strategy advantaged). Episode labels thus apply correct direction to uninformative steps---a granularity mismatch, not label error---explaining why step-level router training approaches chance performance (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
\emph{Self-supervised} approaches learn tool invocation from loss signals: Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2023} inserts API calls filtered by perplexity reduction; ToolACE \cite{toolace-2025} curates synthetic data for function-calling accuracy.
\emph{Token-level} methods embed tools into the model vocabulary: ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as special tokens resolved at inference.
\emph{Task-level} decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules: ART \cite{art-2023} generates tool-augmented programs; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2023} composes heterogeneous modules per subtask.
\emph{RL-based} methods learn routing policies from reward: ReTool \cite{retool-2025} discovers when code execution is preferable to chain-of-thought.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: \citet{tool-overuse-illusion-2026} find tool overuse correlates with model uncertainty; \citet{when2tool-2026} show tool-call decisions are encoded in hidden states; \citet{tool-use-tax-2026} quantify a ``tool-use tax''; \citet{to-call-or-not-2026} propose a necessity-utility-affordability framework. Our decomposition provides environment-grounded evidence: in ScienceWorld, 78.9\% of routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. In the LLM era, CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} adapts reasoning depth per step, ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects among reasoning effort levels, Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths, and MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn routing.
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models.
These optimize \emph{how} to route; our framework reveals \emph{why} routing helps: in interactive environments, the dominant signal is catastrophic-failure avoidance.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations for RL agents by perturbing individual actions within a fixed policy \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}.
Our approach differs in both purpose and granularity: we execute two complete episodes under different routing strategies, producing prospective paired comparisons that isolate routing signal \emph{before} any router is trained.

\paragraph{LLM Agent Environments and Reasoning.}
ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \cite{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary household environment; AgentBench \cite{agentbench-2024} offers systematic evaluation. \citet{zhou2024webarena} document common failure modes (premature termination, hallucinated actions) in web-based agents, consistent with our finding that state hallucination is the primary failure mechanism. ReAct \cite{react-2023} serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \cite{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection; LATS \cite{lats-2024} adds tree search.

\paragraph{Safe RL.}
Death avoidance as an implicit routing signal connects to safe RL. Constrained MDPs \citep{altman1999constrained} formalize safety through state or cost constraints; our mechanism can be viewed as an implicit absorbing-state constraint. Risk-sensitive RL \citep{mihatsch2002risk} optimizes CVaR-type objectives; our framework diagnoses when such risk-sensitivity emerges rather than prescribing it. Classical methods \citep{garcia2015comprehensive,tamar2015cvar,achiam2017cpo,crpo-2021,moldovan2012safe} require explicit constraint specification; our routing achieves safety implicitly through computation mode selection. \citet{ruan2024failures} provide a taxonomy of LM agent failures identifying catastrophic actions as a distinct category, consistent with our finding. SafeRoute \citep{saferoute-2025} applies adaptive model selection for safety guardrails.

\section{Conclusion}

We introduced an episode-level counterfactual diagnostic framework for decomposing routing signal in modular LLM agents, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy (97.4\% tie rate) through paired pure-strategy comparison with penalty-independent (label fraction) and penalty-dependent (DAF) metrics. Applied to ScienceWorld, 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels stem from death asymmetry---robust across four intervention axes, cross-model replication (Llama-3.1-8B, DAF${=}88.2\%$), and boundary probes (MATH, ALFWorld). The diagnostic value is practical: our 200 GPU-hour router training could not outperform the always-deterministic baseline, an outcome a 50-episode pilot would have predicted.

The framework applies to any environment with episode-level evaluation and binary catastrophic outcomes. Future work should target intermediate death regimes (NetHack combat tasks), develop methods to disentangle label noise from fundamental non-learnability in step-level routing, and extend to mixed-strategy routing and stronger backbones.

